{
  "$comment": "How an item's property modifiers combine with a hero's stats. The API gives every item property a `provided_property_type` — the engine's own modifier vocabulary — and that is what we key off. The property's own `label` is used for the display name, so this file only needs to say what a modifier does, not what it is called. Hero base stats use different keys entirely (max_health, base_health_regen, ...), so `heroStat` names the base stat a modifier adds to. A modifier with no `heroStat` is a flat modifier: it is summed and shown, but there is no base to add it to.",
  "groups": [
    { "id": "health", "label": "Health" },
    { "id": "spirit", "label": "Spirit" },
    { "id": "weapon", "label": "Weapon" },
    { "id": "defence", "label": "Defence" },
    { "id": "ability", "label": "Ability" },
    { "id": "movement", "label": "Movement" },
    { "id": "sustain", "label": "Sustain" },
    { "id": "utility", "label": "Utility" }
  ],
  "rail": {
    "$comment": "The seven stats the theorycraft rail shows while building. Each names the modifier types that feed it, so the rail reads the build by modifier type rather than by the display label — several modifier types share a label, and the label depends on which property an item happens to use.",
    "health": {
      "label": "Max Health",
      "modifiers": ["MODIFIER_VALUE_HEALTH_MAX", "MODIFIER_VALUE_HEALTH_MAX_PERCENT", "MODIFIER_VALUE_BASE_HEALTH_PERCENT"]
    },
    "spirit": {
      "label": "Spirit Power",
      "modifiers": ["MODIFIER_VALUE_TECH_POWER"]
    },
    "weapon": {
      "label": "Weapon Damage",
      "modifiers": ["MODIFIER_VALUE_WEAPON_POWER"]
    },
